  "text": "Who it is for: Me, and anyone who wants an assistant that can touch their machine without shipping their desktop to a company. Chat assistants answer and stop. If I asked one to open a file, rename it and send it, it wrote me instructions for doing that myself. What it does: Jeffrey listens, picks a route, calls the right tool, runs it on macOS and answers in the language it was asked in. One sentence in, an action out. Five systems coordinate behind it, and it keeps working with the network down because the local model is not a demo mode. What changed along the way: My first instinct when Jeffrey picked the wrong action was that the model was not good enough. It was not the model. The tools I had handed it were vaguely described, so it was choosing between options that overlapped, and no amount of model quality fixes an ambiguous menu. Rewriting the tool definitions to be narrow and unambiguous fixed more than swapping models ever did. What he learned: When the AI acts instead of answering, the tools matter more than the model. A weaker model with well shaped tools beat a stronger one with vague ones.",
